<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Demak Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1475 CE – 1554 CE (approx.) · capital Demak</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Muslim sultanate of Java, founded by Raden Patah on the north coast, which replaced Majapahit as the island's leading power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Demak was a port on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>. In the late 15th century its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-patah">Raden Patah</a>, described in Javanese tradition as a son of the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> king and a Chinese mother, threw off Majapahit's overlordship. Supported by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Songo</a>, the nine saints, he made Demak the centre of a coalition of Muslim coastal states.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-demak">Demak's Great Mosque</a>, whose pillars are said to have been made by the saints themselves, became the holiest site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pati-unus">Pati Unus</a> led fleets against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese Malacca</a> in 1513. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trenggana">Sultan Trenggana</a> expanded inland and west, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom</a> in 1527 and conquering much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trenggana">Trenggana</a> died during a campaign at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panarukan">Panarukan</a> in 1546, and a succession war followed in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arya-penangsang">Arya Penangsang</a> killed rival princes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadiwijaya">Hadiwijaya of Pajang</a> defeated him and moved power inland, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pajang-sultanate">Pajang Sultanate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/demak-sultanate.jpg" alt="Demak Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Mhatopzz · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MUS_Koin_Sultan_Pangeran_Raden_Fatah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1475 CE – 1554 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Demak</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Raden Patah, Pati Unus, Trenggana</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Demak, Demak</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1514&amp;focus=demak-sultanate" aria-label="Demak Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7743 2435 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Demak Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8063 2691L8112 2721M8063 2691L7941 2659M8063 2691L8065 2717M8063 2691L8181 2558"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8112 2721h0M7941 2659h0M8065 2717h0M8181 2558h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8063 2691h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8063 2691h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1514&amp;focus=demak-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
